1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,023
Lesson 5.6.

2
00:00:02,403 --> 00:00:04,207
Analog non-idealities.

3
00:00:05,317 --> 00:00:09,504
On the blackboard, a
coupling is exactly 0.37.

4
00:00:09,884 --> 00:00:13,633
On an analog chip, that number
is stored with few bits, each

5
00:00:13,633 --> 00:00:17,633
transistor is slightly different
from its neighbor, the circuits

6
00:00:17,633 --> 00:00:21,195
are not perfectly linear and
the current itself is noisy.

7
00:00:21,575 --> 00:00:25,143
The practical question is how
much these imperfections distort

8
00:00:25,143 --> 00:00:28,309
the distribution we sample,
and how they are corrected.

9
00:00:29,417 --> 00:00:32,415
Take a ten-spin Ising model with random

10
00:00:32,415 --> 00:00:35,645
couplings, which we can enumerate exactly.

11
00:00:36,025 --> 00:00:39,514
Let's quantize the couplings
to b bits and measure the KL

12
00:00:39,514 --> 00:00:43,126
divergence between the true
and the quantized distribution.

13
00:00:43,506 --> 00:00:47,644
With two bits, the KL is
1.8: it is a different model.

14
00:00:48,024 --> 00:00:49,875
With three, 0.21.

15
00:00:50,255 --> 00:00:52,384
With four, 0.07.

16
00:00:52,764 --> 00:00:56,620
With six bits it drops to two thousandths,
and with eight it is negligible.

17
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,907
Each additional bit reduces
the error to a fraction.

18
00:01:01,017 --> 00:01:05,473
Mismatch between devices acts
as a random error in each field.

19
00:01:05,853 --> 00:01:10,304
If each pbit has a Gaussian
offset in its field with standard

20
00:01:10,304 --> 00:01:14,537
deviation 0.05, the average
KL is about eight thousandths.

21
00:01:14,917 --> 00:01:18,790
With 0.1 it rises to
0.03, and with 0.2, to

22
00:01:18,790 --> 00:01:22,843
0.13: it grows as the
square of the mismatch.

23
00:01:23,223 --> 00:01:27,348
That is why each pbit is calibrated
individually, as we saw in X0.

24
00:01:28,450 --> 00:01:29,897
Two more error sources.

25
00:01:30,277 --> 00:01:35,880
Nonlinearity: the circuit that sums the
local field is not perfectly linear, and the

26
00:01:35,880 --> 00:01:40,682
pbit transfer curve is not exactly a
sigmoid; according to the syllabus,

27
00:01:41,062 --> 00:01:46,719
a comparator with shot noise produces a
probit-like curve, close but different.

28
00:01:47,099 --> 00:01:51,693
And shot noise, due to the discrete
nature of charge, adds fluctuations

29
00:01:51,693 --> 00:01:55,769
that are not thermal and that
change the effective temperature.

30
00:01:56,149 --> 00:01:58,906
All of this is modeled,
measured and compensated.

31
00:02:00,017 --> 00:02:03,183
An elegant mitigation
is stochastic rounding.

32
00:02:03,563 --> 00:02:07,787
Instead of rounding a value to
the nearest level, it is rounded

33
00:02:07,787 --> 00:02:11,610
up or down with probability
proportional to the distance.

34
00:02:11,990 --> 00:02:15,932
Each rounding is noisy, but
its expected value is exact.

35
00:02:16,312 --> 00:02:21,364
If we average several independent
roundings, the mean coupling

36
00:02:21,364 --> 00:02:25,765
error drops from 0.1 to 0.02
with sixteen repetitions,

37
00:02:26,145 --> 00:02:30,711
while deterministic rounding
stays at 0.09 forever.

38
00:02:31,817 --> 00:02:36,350
Another idea is the conservative
quantizer: choosing the range and scale of

39
00:02:36,350 --> 00:02:41,004
the levels so that the large couplings,
which weigh most in the distribution,

40
00:02:41,004 --> 00:02:44,691
are represented well, even if
the small ones lose resolution.

41
00:02:45,071 --> 00:02:48,446
And at the output, as we will
see in unit eight, at least

42
00:02:48,446 --> 00:02:51,704
eight bits are needed so that
gradients do not degrade.

43
00:02:52,084 --> 00:02:57,094
Designing a thermodynamic chip is,
to a large extent, an error budget.

44
00:02:58,217 --> 00:03:04,698
In practice one chooses the number of bits
from a target KL, calibrates each device,

45
00:03:04,698 --> 00:03:10,408
uses stochastic rounding to remove
bias and, as we will see in lesson 8.7,

46
00:03:10,788 --> 00:03:13,902
trains the model knowing
the hardware is imperfect.

47
00:03:15,017 --> 00:03:17,995
Frequent mistakes: judging precision by

48
00:03:17,995 --> 00:03:21,431
coupling error instead
of distribution error.

49
00:03:21,811 --> 00:03:26,308
Assuming errors average out,
when deterministic bias does not.

50
00:03:26,688 --> 00:03:28,381
Ignoring shot noise.

51
00:03:28,761 --> 00:03:33,450
And calibrating only once, when drift
with temperature requires recalibration.

52
00:03:34,550 --> 00:03:38,551
In summary: quantization,
mismatch, nonlinearity and

53
00:03:38,551 --> 00:03:41,936
shot noise distort the
sampled distribution.

54
00:03:42,316 --> 00:03:46,636
The KL falls quickly with bits and
grows as the square of the mismatch.

55
00:03:47,016 --> 00:03:50,798
Calibrating, rounding
stochastically and quantizing wisely

56
00:03:50,798 --> 00:03:53,994
make up the error budget
of a thermodynamic chip.

57
00:03:55,117 --> 00:03:59,238
To practice: prove that
stochastic rounding is unbiased.

58
00:03:59,618 --> 00:04:03,180
Explain why the KL grows as
the square of the mismatch.

59
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:06,620
Choose the number of bits for a target KL.

60
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:10,614
And explain how shot noise
changes the effective temperature.
